{
  "info": {
    "_postman_id": "a7f3c1d2-9e4b-4c8a-b5d6-1f2e3a4b5c6d",
    "name": "Task Management System API",
    "description": "Postman collection for the Task Management System (Express + Sequelize/Postgres).\n\n## Base URL\nAll requests use the collection variable `{{baseUrl}}` (default: `http://localhost:5000`).\n\n## How to use\n1. Make sure the server is running (`npm run dev` or `npm start`) and migrations/seeders have been applied.\n2. Set the collection variables `userPassword` and `adminPassword` to the passwords of the seeded accounts (see `seeders/` for the seeded users). No tokens or passwords are stored in this collection.\n3. Run the **Authentication** folder first: `Login (Regular User)` stores its JWT in `{{token}}`, and `Login (Admin)` stores its JWT in `{{adminToken}}`. These are used automatically as Bearer tokens on protected requests.\n4. Run `Create Task` before the requests that reference `{{taskId}}` — its test script captures the created task's ID.\n5. `{{otherTaskId}}` (default `1`) should point to a task owned by a different user than the regular user, so ownership-scoping responses (404) can be demonstrated.\n\n## Endpoints\n| Method | Path | Auth | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| GET | / | none | Health check |\n| POST | /auth/register | none | Register a new user (role `user`) |\n| POST | /auth/login | none | Returns a JWT `token` |\n| POST | /tasks | Bearer | Create a task (owned by caller, status defaults to `Pending`) |\n| GET | /tasks | Bearer | List tasks: regular user sees only own tasks; admin sees all |\n| GET | /tasks/:id | Bearer | Owner or admin; non-owners get 404 |\n| PUT | /tasks/:id | Bearer | Update title/description — owner-scoped for every role |\n| PATCH | /tasks/:id/status | Bearer + admin | Admin only. Status must be one of: `Pending`, `In Progress`, `Testing`, `Completed` |\n\n## Authorization behavior demonstrated here\n- Unauthenticated or invalid-token requests → `401`.\n- Regular user calling `PATCH /tasks/:id/status` → `403 Admin access required`.\n- `GET /tasks` as a regular user returns only their own tasks; as admin it returns every user's tasks.\n- `GET /tasks/:id` / `PUT /tasks/:id` for a task owned by someone else → `404` (even for admin, updates are owner-scoped).",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
